+## 3. Design, and what each part is worth
+
+Measured on real output unless marked as a bound. The stage-1 review (`reviews/R01.md` of the pass) found that at
+the zstd -3 point KLZH's parse is level with zstd's (-1.4% to +0.6% under one cost model) and the whole gap is in
+the entropy stage.
+
+1. **One token per sequence**: offset code x min(literal length, 3); literal-length codes only from 3; match-length
+   codes in two streams (after a repeat offset, after a new one). Most sequences cost two symbols instead of three.
+   With khuf chunks on a 64 KiB grid this alone gave 72,260,473 (level 1) and 70,010,861 (level 3) on the 13 files
+   (commit `f08302a5`): -1.74% and -2.11% against codec 29.
+2. **Sub-blocks by input position**: every stream may start a chunk where the input crosses a multiple of 64 KiB;
+   neighbours are joined while one table is estimated to cost no more than two plus a penalty (64 bytes for
+   literals, 32 for code streams). Integer estimates only: the same choice on every host.
+3. **tANS for the code streams**, written for Kolmo from Duda's construction (arXiv:1311.2540, tabled variant): 2^5
+   to 2^11 states, bit-reversal spread, a compact count header, two states over one bit stream under 256 symbols and
+   four states over two bit streams from there. Four files, level 3: 12,264,540 -> 12,224,397 (-0.33%) over khuf
+   chunks. Table size: 2^10 at most (2^11 is 0.003% smaller, 2^9 0.12% larger on five files), about one state per
+   four symbols.
+4. **Finer match-length codes** (48 direct values, then two codes per octave): 56 KiB on nci as an entropy bound
+   taken before it was built.
+5. **Coded low offset bits**: the low four bits of new offsets of 16 or more become a tANS stream when their
+   histogram says so (a per-block flag). Bound on mozilla: 985,335 -> 660,568 bytes of those bits.
+6. **Small inputs**: predefined tANS tables (2^9 states, fitted by `gen_pre.c` on 2 to 32 KiB blocks of ten Silesia
+   files, part of the format) for code chunks under 1024 symbols, and tANS over bytes for literal chunks under 4096.
+7. **khuf blocks for long code chunks** (levels 1-4): section 4.
+
+Rejected alternatives, measured: a joint symbol over (literal-length class, match-length class, offset class) is
+larger than separate streams on six files (webster: 4,412,789 against 4,148,736 bytes of entropy); tables per 32 KiB
+with khuf's table format cost more than they save on nci, dickens, osdb, webster; conditioning contexts on the
+sequence codes save 4.7% on nci as a bound but 0.2% to 0.5% on text and cost decode time (not in the fast tier).
+
+## 4. Encoder choices that trade size for decode speed
+
+khuf decodes two symbols per table lookup on blocks of 4096 symbols or more; tANS decodes one symbol per state step.
+Mode 3 of a code chunk therefore means "khuf block" from 1024 symbols on (below it: the predefined table). Six files
+(mozilla, samba, nci, webster, x-ray, dickens), level 3, `dec_time` best of 7, commit `c0e30b02`:
+
+| policy | bytes | entropy decode of the streams | whole decode |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| codec 29 | 49,499,800 | 41.4 ms | 164.9 ms |
+| tANS only (`KZ2_HUF_BIAS 0`) | 47,622,616 | 61.8 ms | 176.3 ms |
+| khuf from 16384 symbols when within 1/64 of the tANS estimate (**kept, levels 1-4**) | 47,680,142 (+0.12%) | 54.2 ms | 170.5 ms |
+| the same from 4096 symbols | 47,688,545 | 55.2 ms | 172.5 ms |
+| within 1/16, from 16384 | 47,693,002 | 53.5 ms | 167.2 ms |
+| from 65536 | 47,666,197 | 55.4 ms | 168.4 ms |
+
+On text the stream decode reaches codec 29's (webster 11.6 -> 8.2 ms against 8.1). The 13-file totals moved from
+71,481,703 / 69,034,738 (tANS only, commit `a154f538`) to 71,541,209 / 69,115,455 (+0.08% / +0.12%). **This is a
+judgement call for the owner**: `KZ2_HUF_BIAS 0` gives the smaller files with the same decoder; nothing in the
+format changes either way.
+
+The penalty for one more table (decoders build a table per chunk), six files, level 3 (working tree between
+`a154f538` and `c0e30b02`, khuf chunks from 4096 symbols):
+
+| literal / code penalty (bytes) | bytes | stream decode |
+|---|---|---|
+| 64 / 32 (**kept**) | 39,275,205 | 51.8 ms |
+| 128 / 64 | 39,323,771 (+0.12%) | 49.4 ms |
+| 256 / 128 | 39,410,955 (+0.35%) | 46.8 ms |
+| 512 / 256 | 39,559,453 (+0.72%) | 44.0 ms |
+
+(mozilla, samba, ooffice, osdb, x-ray, dickens for this table.) Nearly all of the time is mozilla's.
+
+## 5. Where codec 32 is slower to decode, and why
+
+Text and markup decode at codec 29's speed or faster; heterogeneous binary data is slower (mozilla, ooffice, mr, sao:
+table of section 2). Measured causes on mozilla (callgrind, 8 MiB, level 3):
+
+- **Table builds.** mozilla's statistics change often, so its 13 blocks hold 335 khuf literal chunks and 1,165
+  code chunks at level 3 (`chunks`, tANS only), each with a table to build. In the profiled variant (khuf chunks from
+  4096 symbols) `kolmo_khuf_decompress` spends about 16M of its 55M instructions building tables (`dt`, `d1`, `d2`,
+  `kh_codes`) and they hold a third of the decoder's mispredicted branches (135k of 411k). With chunks joined to
+  their maximum the stream decode of the whole file falls from 29.2 to 20.3 ms and the file grows 2.5%. khuf's
+  table construction is outside this claim (the khuf owner's kernels); a cheaper build there would help codec 32
+  directly.
+- **One symbol per lookup.** The tANS loop costs about 9 micro-operations per symbol; khuf's two-symbol loop about
+  half per symbol. This is what section 4's khuf chunks buy back.
+- **Two data-dependent branches more per sequence** (literal length in the token or in the stream; which
+  match-length stream): 322k against 164k mispredicted branches for the whole decode of 8 MiB of mozilla at level 1
+  (callgrind's predictor model, codec 32 against codec 29). See the negative result below.
+
+## 6. Negative results (kept)
+
+- **Branch-free sequence loop.** Selecting the literal-length and match-length sources with masks, and a fully
+  branch-free variant with a four-entry repeat table, were slower than the branchy loop on this host: natbench best
+  of 9, five files, level 3, three rounds, MB/s: branchy 904 / 927 / 933, masks with branchy offsets 840 / 836 / 817,
+  fully branch-free 770 / 803 / 745 (codec 29 in the same runs: 1033 / 1036 / 1005). On 8 MiB of mozilla the fully
+  branch-free loop halved the mispredictions (311k -> 156k) and raised the instruction count from 83.4M to 103.5M; a
+  likely cause of the loss is that the literal length then sits on the loop-carried path of the output pointer.
+- **Packed table entries and register-held states** in the tANS loop (commit `ee0c7ea3`): the generated loop went
+  from about 18 to about 14 instructions per symbol; stream decode of seven files 64.6 -> 63.3 ms. Kept, but it is
+  2%, not the 10% the instruction count suggested: the symbol loop is not where mozilla's time is.
+- **Penalties for more tables** buy little (section 4): 0.35% of size for 2% of decode time.
+- **Encoder on 4 KiB blocks** is still 0.67x of codec 29 at level 1 (and codec 29 is 0.43x of zstd -1 there, because
+  the parse zeroes its full tables per call: the planned `klz-fast-parse-speed`). The tANS encoder needs two passes
+  per chunk (states backwards, bits forwards) and builds a table per chunk.
+- **Size on nci, xml, samba, x-ray** at level 3 is still above zstd -3 (section 1).
+
+## 7. Compatibility and tests
+
+- **rc3 frames:** unchanged. Codecs 28 and 29 write the same bytes as rc3 (hashes taken from the rc3 sources,
+  `tests/klz_rc3_hashes.h`); the default output is unchanged (`python3 benchmark/determinism/check.py build/kolmo
+  DIR`: 122 frames OK); with `KOLMO_NATIVE=lz` all 26 frames of the end-to-end set are byte-identical to the rc3
+  binary's, and the rc3 binary's frames decode with this build.
+- **New frames:** a frame holding codec 32 is refused by an rc3 reader (13 of 13 `balanced` frames: "corrupt or
+  unsupported input"). It is written only under `KOLMO_NATIVE=lz2`.
+- **Frozen frames:** `tests/klzh2_frames.h` (seven frames written when the codec was introduced) and
+  `tests/klzh2_frames2.h` (one with khuf code chunks) must keep decoding; between them they hold every chunk kind.
+- **Hostile input:** `tests/test_native.c` (round trips at all nine levels on inputs built to reach every part of
+  the format, every truncation, bit flips and random bytes in headers and tables, wrong sizes) under ASan and UBSan
+  (`make check-native`); `fuzz/fuzz_native_lz.c` reaches the decoders of codecs 28, 29 and 32 directly.
+
+## 8. Cost
+
+- **Binary:** stripped `build/kolmo` 3,500,808 -> 3,537,672 bytes (+36,864); text segment 3,485,893 -> 3,520,253.
+- **Memory:** the decoder allocates the literals (at most the block) and four code bytes per sequence (at most the
+  block); `blk_dec_need` declares block + 2 x block + 2 MiB for codec 32. The encoder allocates as codec 29 plus two
+  code bytes and three scratch bytes per sequence, and 129 KiB of khuf scratch instead of about one block. Measured
+  peak RSS of the CLI: table in section 2.
+
+## 9. Not verified
+
+- Speeds on the Apple M5, and the generated code of the tANS loop under clang on AArch64 (the GCC-only attribute on
+  `kza_decode` has no effect there).
+- Graph leaves and table columns in isolation (only through the CLI totals above and the 4 / 32 KiB block runs).
+- Coverage-guided fuzzing: libFuzzer's runtime is not installed on this host. The target was run with the
+  repository's local mutation driver under GCC's ASan/UBSan instead (`gcc -g -O1 -fsanitize=address,undefined
+  -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined -Isrc fuzz/fuzz_native_lz.c fuzz/run_local.c src/algo/klz.c src/algo/khuf.c`,
+  then `run_native_lz 300000 SEEDS/*`): 72 seeds (valid blocks of codecs 28, 29 and 32 cut from Silesia files, 88
+  bytes to 458 KB, and raw inputs for the round-trip arm), each run as is and under 500 mutations, then 300,000
+  random inputs: no report, exit 0 (commit `ee0c7ea3`).
+- Big-endian hosts are refused at compile time, as for the rest of `klz.c`.
